(Regression test: PRINT_HEX_LOWER_PADDED, PRINT_HEX_UPPER_PADDED,
PRINT_BINARY_PADDED, and PRINT_OCTAL_PADDED did not exist at all before
this fix - format strings like "{n:6x}" (width, no leading zero) would
fail at NASM assembly time with an undefined-symbol error for any base
other than decimal.)

A number called "n" is 255.
A number called "b" is 10.
A number called "o" is 15.

(Space-padded - the previously-missing macros)
Print "{n:6x}".
Print "{n:6X}".
Print "{b:8b}".
Print "{o:6o}".

(Width smaller than the digits - no padding needed)
Print "{n:2x}".

(Zero-padded siblings - confirm the refactor didn't change their
existing, already-working behavior)
Print "{n:06x}".
Print "{n:06X}".
Print "{b:08b}".
Print "{o:06o}".
